  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2510.18551v2</id>
    <title>SOCIA-Nabla: Textual Gradient Meets Multi-Agent Orchestration for Automated Simulator Generation</title>
    <updated>2025-11-10T14:12:42Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.18551v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.18551v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>In this paper, we present SOCIA-Nabla, an end-to-end, agentic framework that treats simulator construction asinstance optimization over code within a textual computation graph. Specialized LLM-driven agents are embedded as graph nodes, and a workflow manager executes a loss-driven loop: code synthesis -&gt; execution -&gt; evaluation -&gt; code repair. The optimizer performs Textual-Gradient Descent (TGD), while human-in-the-loop interaction is reserved for task-spec confirmation, minimizing expert effort and keeping the code itself as the trainable object. Across three CPS tasks, i.e., User Modeling, Mask Adoption, and Personal Mobility, SOCIA-Nabla attains state-of-the-art overall accuracy. By unifying multi-agent orchestration with a loss-aligned optimization view, SOCIA-Nabla converts brittle prompt pipelines into reproducible, constraint-aware simulator code generation that scales across domains and simulation granularities. This work is under review, and we will release the code soon.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-10-21T12:00:00Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>superseded by newest version of arXiv:2505.12006</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
